<form class="{{cssClass}} steading-sheet" autocomplete="off">
	<div class="sheet-wrapper steading-sheet-wrapper">

		<div class="steading-header">
			<div class="steading-header-name">
				<h1 class="steading-title">Stonetop</h1>
			</div>
			{{!-- What the steading is still owed, and what it still owes: one glyph per
			      unresolved thing, beside the title. The rule for what earns a place here is in
			      module/actors/steading/steading-holds.js, and it is deliberately narrow: an
			      icon shows only while something is UNRESOLVED and disappears when it resolves.
			      So instant effects, held pools (Favor, Blessing, and the rest) and permanent
			      passives are all absent, and the row means one specific thing — if a glyph is
			      lit, somebody has something to do. Usually that is nothing, and the whole block
			      is gone.

			      Each row is an empty <span> wearing its drawing as a CSS mask, exactly like the
			      weather glyph further along this header, so one file takes the header's ink in
			      both themes. Which drawing is which is in assets/icons/holds/ATTRIBUTION.md.

			      A row that leads somewhere is a <button> for a GM and the same glyph as a plain
			      readout for everyone else: a player should still see that the watch wants
			      feeding without being given a control that would only refuse them. Complementary
			      {{#if}}/{{#unless}} rather than {{else}}, as everything else in this file does
			      (steading-section-placement.test.js asserts the file is free of {{else}}).

			      `role="img"` with the label on the wrapper and the glyph hidden: an empty span
			      painting a masked background has no text to announce. --}}
			{{#if stonetop.holds.length}}
			<div class="steading-header-holds" role="group" aria-label="Outstanding at Stonetop">
				{{#each stonetop.holds}}
				{{#if interactive}}
				<button type="button"
				        class="steading-hold steading-hold--{{tone}} steading-hold--{{icon}}"
				        data-action="{{action}}"
				        aria-label="{{label}}"
				        data-tooltip="{{tooltip}}" data-tooltip-direction="DOWN">
					<span class="steading-hold-glyph" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</button>
				{{/if}}
				{{#unless interactive}}
				<span class="steading-hold steading-hold--{{tone}} steading-hold--{{icon}}"
				      role="img"
				      aria-label="{{label}}"
				      data-tooltip="{{tooltip}}" data-tooltip-direction="DOWN">
					<span class="steading-hold-glyph" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</span>
				{{/unless}}
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}
			{{!-- The campaign's clock, beside the title: the season the table is playing in and
			      the year it belongs to, stamped by the Seasons Change move (see
			      module/seasons/current-season.js). Both halves always show: a world that has
			      not turned a season yet is still in the season it started in, so an un-stamped
			      clock falls back on DEFAULT_SEASON and the current year rather than naming
			      half of itself.

			      A GM clicking it makes the move (`_onSeasonsChange`); correcting the clock
			      without rolling is a door at the bottom of that dialog, because the move
			      happens four times a year and the correction about once a campaign. A player
			      gets the same readout as plain text. Rendered as a <button> for the GM rather
			      than a clickable <div> so it is keyboard-reachable and announces itself.

			      Complementary `{{#if}}` / `{{#unless}}` rather than one `{{#if}}/{{else}}`,
			      the same shape the layout guards below use — this file's markup is asserted
			      to be branch-free of `{{else}}` so that the classic/modern split can never
			      become two whole copies of the sheet (tests/actors/steading/
			      steading-section-placement.test.js). The readout itself is a partial, so the
			      two wrappers cannot drift apart. --}}
			{{!-- The clock and the sky it is under, as one block parked at the header's right
			      edge. The WRAPPER is what carries the auto margin now: the season keeps its own
			      (it is asserted, and it is what the readout has always been positioned by), but
			      two auto left margins in one flex row SPLIT the free space rather than stacking,
			      which would strand the weather glyph in the middle of the header. Inside a
			      content-sized wrapper the season's margin has no slack to take and is inert. --}}
			<div class="steading-header-clock">
				{{!-- What the sky is doing: the last weather the GM posted from the Weather
				      picker, or fine weather on a world that has never set one (see
				      module/seasons/current-weather.js).

				      The same two wrappers the clock beside it uses, for the same reason. A GM
				      clicking it opens the Weather picker — the window that decides this glyph,
				      so the readout is also the way back to the thing that set it. A player gets
				      the readout as plain text: the picker rolls, posts to chat and writes the
				      steading, none of which is theirs to do.

				      The hover carries the row's own line and nothing else, on both branches
				      alike: the glyph says what kind of day it is and this says which day, and a
				      hover that also explained the click would bury the one thing it was there
				      to say. What the GM's click does is said by the pointer and by the picker
				      it opens. The season beside it names its own action because its hover has
				      nothing else to carry — a year the chip already shows in full.

				      The glyph itself is an empty <span> wearing the drawing as a CSS mask, hung
				      off the sky's modifier class in stonetop.css — the same way the tab rail
				      wears its icons, and for the same reason: one file then takes the header's
				      ink in both themes and its dimmer tone before anyone has set the weather.
				      Which drawing is which is in assets/icons/weather/ATTRIBUTION.md.

				      `role="img"` with the label on the wrapper and the glyph hidden: an empty
				      span painting a masked background has no text for a screen reader to read,
				      so it announces nothing at all. The GM's <button> takes `aria-label` for the
				      same reason — it has no text of its own to be named by. --}}
				{{#if stonetop.isGM}}
				<button type="button"
				        class="{{stonetop.currentWeather.classes}} steading-header-weather--set"
				        data-action="set-current-weather"
				        aria-label="{{stonetop.currentWeather.label}}"
				        data-tooltip="{{stonetop.currentWeather.text}}">
					<span class="steading-header-weather-glyph" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</button>
				{{/if}}
				{{#unless stonetop.isGM}}
				<span class="{{stonetop.currentWeather.classes}}"
				      role="img"
				      aria-label="{{stonetop.currentWeather.label}}"
				      data-tooltip="{{stonetop.currentWeather.text}}">
					<span class="steading-header-weather-glyph" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</span>
				{{/unless}}

				{{#if stonetop.isGM}}
				<button type="button" class="steading-header-season steading-header-season--set"
				        data-action="set-current-season"
				        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.steading.season.setTooltip"}}">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-header-season" season=stonetop.currentSeason}}
				</button>
				{{/if}}
				{{#unless stonetop.isGM}}
				<div class="steading-header-season">
					{{> "stonetop.steading-header-season" season=stonetop.currentSeason}}
				</div>
				{{/unless}}
			</div>
		</div>

		{{!-- CLASSIC only: the stat band is a permanent strip above the tab strip, the way it
		      was before the icon rail took the sheet's right edge. The SAME partial the Overview
		      tab mounts in the modern layout - only the position differs, and the two guards are
		      complementary so only one ever renders. It must be invoked at TOP LEVEL:
		      steading-stats-bar.hbs reaches up with `../stonetop.edit.*`, so mounting it inside
		      an {{#each}} or with a `this=` override silently renders the band read-only. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.classicLayout}}{{> "stonetop.steading-stats-bar"}}{{/if}}

		{{!-- One <nav> for both layouts, differing by a class and a single entry - not two navs
		      behind {{#if}}/{{else}}, which is two copies of a six-entry list to keep in step.
		      The same shape the character sheet uses.

		      MODERN wears `stonetop-tab-rail`, which is what tab-rail.js keys off to lift this
		      nav onto the window frame (so its position here is arbitrary) and what every
		      `:not(.stonetop-tab-rail)` in the horizontal-strip skin excludes.

		      CLASSIC gets one entry fewer: Homefront Moves is the SIDEBAR there, so it has
		      no page of its own, and the `moves` panel below is dropped with it - a `.tab` with
		      no nav entry is dead markup that still costs a render. A user sitting on `moves`
		      when the setting flips is handled by core: Tabs#activate falls back to the first
		      nav item when the stored name is absent, and stores the fallback.

		      Homefront Moves sits second in modern: it used to be a sidebar visible from every
		      tab, so it is the one page reached most often. --}}
		<nav class="sheet-tabs tabs{{#unless stonetop.classicLayout}} stonetop-tab-rail{{/unless}}"
		     data-group="primary" aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.sheet.tabs.railLabel"}}">
			{{> "stonetop.tab-nav-item" tab="overview"     label=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.overview")}}
			{{#unless stonetop.classicLayout}}{{> "stonetop.tab-nav-item" tab="moves" label=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.moves")}}{{/unless}}
			{{> "stonetop.tab-nav-item" tab="neighbors"    label=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.neighbors")}}
			{{> "stonetop.tab-nav-item" tab="improvements" label=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.improvements")}}
			{{!-- Threats & Dangers and Sites used to sit here. They are the GM's own prep,
			      written for one pair of eyes, so they moved to the GM Toolkit sheet. Their
			      STORAGE did not move: both are still journal pages filed under this steading
			      (module/journal/gm-prep-page-store.js), so nothing about a world's existing
			      prep changed, only where the GM reads it. --}}
			{{> "stonetop.tab-nav-item" tab="notes"        label=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.notes")}}
		</nav>

		<div class="stonetop-sheet-layout steading-sheet-layout stonetop-sheet-layout--own-seam">
			<section class="sheet-body">
				{{> "stonetop.steading-tab-overview"}}
				{{#unless stonetop.classicLayout}}{{> "stonetop.steading-tab-moves"}}{{/unless}}
				{{> "stonetop.steading-tab-neighbors"}}
				{{> "stonetop.steading-tab-improvements"}}
				{{> "stonetop.steading-tab-notes"}}
			</section>

			{{!-- The sidebar and the moves TAB above are the same content in two shapes, so
			      their guards must stay complementary or Homefront Moves renders twice. --}}
			{{#if stonetop.classicLayout}}{{> "stonetop.steading-moves-sidebar"}}{{/if}}
		</div>

	</div>
</form>
